    <description>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "blocks": {
    "contentData": [],
    "settingsData": [],
    "expose": [],
    "Layout": {}
  },
  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EBuildings \u003Cstrong\u003Emust \u003C/strong\u003Eaddress the street or public space (including green spaces) with a primary elevation, typically defined by the main entrance and fenestration. This approach helps to create an engaging streetscene and enable effective passive surveillance. \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExceptions:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHighly Urban Area Type (80\u002B dph): In high-density areas where multi-occupancy buildings are common, the primary elevation \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Eface the street or open space. However, it is acceptable for the main entrance to be located on a secondary elevation if active frontage is maintained on the street-facing side through other ground-floor uses that overlook the street or space \u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EEmployment Area Type: In Employment Zones, Sites, and Special Policy Areas, buildings \u003Cstrong\u003Eshould \u003C/strong\u003Esimilarly have their primary elevation addressing the street. Whilst the main entrance may be positioned on a secondary elevation, active frontage must still be provided along the street. This can be achieved through ground-floor uses such as visitor parking, drop-off points, disabled bays, public transport facilities, or public spaces that serve multiple user groups and activities\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </description>
    <developmentScales>
      <Value><![CDATA[[
  "Minor Residential Applications (1-9 homes)",
  "Major Residential Applications (10\u002B homes)",
  "Minor Employment Applications",
